From e952905e146516e84d36432cf9264a1130a6cead Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:28:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] Bluetooth: hci_conn: fix SCO connection reuse

When a SCO socket is closed and a new one connects to the same device
before the Disconnection Complete of the old link arrives,
hci_connect_sco() hands the dying connection to the new socket:
connect() succeeds without a Setup Synchronous Connection being sent,
and the socket is closed with ECONNABORTED when the event arrives.
This sequence is exercised by PipeWire during an HFP codec change, and
with headsets that answer AT+BCS quickly the new codec never gets a
link.

Patch 1 first fixes the hold a reused connection comes with: a second
connect() to a device whose SCO link is up fails with EBUSY, as it
should, but drops the hold of the socket that owns the link, which then
gets disconnected. Patch 1 keeps the EBUSY and stops the disconnect, as
e06d549fcd4a did for CIS.

Patch 2 fails a connect that would be handed the dying connection with
-ECONNABORTED instead.

Patch 3 closes the window before abort_reason is set: an
hci_conn_timeout() worker that already saw no holder can still abort a
connection that a new connect() has just taken. A connect() that comes
before that worker has started at all still cancels it and keeps the
live link, as it does today; the series does not change that case.

Tested on bluetooth-next 27731ba09195 in QEMU with BlueZ's emulator,
uniprocessor and SMP, with lockdep enabled, and with new sco-tester
cases ("[PATCH BlueZ] tools/sco-tester: Add SCO reconnect tests", sent
alongside). "Reconnect During Disconnect" and "Connect Twice" fail
without the series and pass with it, 3 of 3 runs on uniprocessor, SMP
and KCSAN kernels; "Reconnect After Disconnect" passes on both. The
window patch 3 closes was reproduced by holding hci_conn_timeout() after
it saw no holder (a debug-only change), for a link the host set up and
for one the remote device set up: with patches 1 and 2 the new socket
is still reported connected and then aborted, with patch 3 the connect
fails with ECONNABORTED. sco-tester, iso-tester and l2cap-tester
otherwise give the same results with and without the series, and so
does mgmt-tester. The series has not yet been run on hardware; the bug
was found on a QCA9377 laptop with PipeWire 1.0.5.

The series does not by itself make PipeWire's codec switch succeed:
PipeWire retries a connect that fails with ECONNABORTED at once, still
before the old link is gone, so its retry fails too. A prototype that
instead defers such a connect until the old link has been torn down
lets that retry succeed in the same tests; it builds on this series and
changes connect() semantics, so I can post it as an RFC if that
approach is preferred.

The series is independent of Hitalo Souza's pending SCO setup series
(https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/bluetooth/list/?series=1181908)
and applies with or without it. Jeremy Dean Clifton's pending "hci_core:
Fix queuing disc_work while workqueue is drained" (syzbot's "WARNING in
hci_connect_sco") is on the same path but does not overlap: it changes
hci_conn_drop() in hci_core.h.

For stable: patch 1 applies alone on 6.6.y and 6.12.y, which have the
8a5b0135d4a5 backport and the reuse path but not e6720779ae61; patches
2 and 3 apply on top of it on 6.18.y and 7.2.y. Each of those heads was
built and run with sco-tester without and with the patches it needs:
the cases for its bugs fail without them and pass with them. 6.1.y has
no reuse path, hence the "# 6.6.x" on patch 1.

Trees with e6720779ae61 but without 816fb1590a4c ("Bluetooth: SCO:
serialise sco_conn lifetime against sco_recv_scodata()"), such as
Ubuntu 24.04's 7.0 kernel, need it and its prerequisite abd93c85c8667
("Bluetooth: SCO: give the socket its own sco_conn reference") first.
There, sco_connect() does not put the sco_conn reference that
sco_conn_add() took when sco_chan_add() fails with EBUSY, and that
reference holds the hci_conn: once patch 1 stops the disconnect, the
SCO link stays up after both sockets are closed. On Ubuntu 7.0.0-38's
Bluetooth sources with those two commits, "Reconnect During
Disconnect" and "Connect Twice" fail without the series and pass with
it, uniprocessor and SMP.

